📘 Serialization vs Deserialization (Interview Notes)

| Term | Meaning |
| ---- | ------- |
| Serialization | Object → byte stream |
| Deserialization | Byte stream → object |

────────────────────────────────────────
🔹 Why use it?
- Persistence (save to file/DB)
- Networking (send objects)
- Caching

────────────────────────────────────────
🔹 Requirements
1) Class implements `Serializable` (marker interface — no methods)
2) Prefer explicit `serialVersionUID`
3) Non-serializable referenced fields also need care (or mark `transient`)

────────────────────────────────────────
🔹 Key keywords
- `Serializable` → allow serialization
- `serialVersionUID` → version compatibility during deserialize
- `transient` → skip field (e.g. password) → becomes default (`null`/0) after deserialize

────────────────────────────────────────
🔹 Marker interface
Interface with no methods/fields used only as a tag.
Examples: `Serializable`, `Cloneable`, `Remote`
Modern alternative in some cases: annotations.

────────────────────────────────────────
🎤 Interview traps
1) Without `Serializable` → `NotSerializableException`
2) Changing class without updating/keeping UID can cause `InvalidClassException`
3) `transient` fields are not restored
4) `static` fields are not part of object state serialization (class-level)
5) Constructor is not called during normal deserialization (object rebuilt from stream)
